// geometry-demo — the \geometry primitive, which sets the page frame the way LaTeX's
// geometry package does, but with texish name:value options instead of a key=value list.
//
// This document sets a deliberately asymmetric frame — a wide two-inch left margin, a
// one-inch right margin, and a tall top margin — so the effect is plain on the page: the
// text block is pushed right and down from where a default 1in-all-round letter page would
// put it. \geometry is given in the "preamble", before any content, and its settings are
// global, so they hold for every page.

\use{document}

\geometry paper:letter left:2in right:1in top:1.5in bottom:1in

\title{The \texttt{geometry} Page Frame}
\author{The texish engine}
\date{Version 0.19.9}
\maketitle

\section{What it controls}

The \texttt{geometry} primitive resolves a page frame and sets the variables the page
builder reads: the physical sheet (\texttt{paperwidth}, \texttt{paperheight}), the text
size (\texttt{hsize}, \texttt{vsize}), and the top-left offset of the text block
(\texttt{hoffset}, \texttt{voffset}). Every option is a \texttt{name:value} pair, and the
far margin is always whatever is left over, so the frame closes by construction.

This page was set with

\begin{quote}
\noindent\texttt{\\geometry paper:letter left:2in right:1in top:1.5in bottom:1in}
\end{quote}

\noindent which is why the body sits two inches in from the left and an inch and a half
down from the top, with an inch of space at the right and foot.

\section{The options}

\begin{description}
\item[paper] a named sheet --- \texttt{letter}, \texttt{legal}, \texttt{a3},
\texttt{a4} or \texttt{a5}. \texttt{paperwidth} and \texttt{paperheight} set the sheet
explicitly instead.
\item[landscape] swap the sheet to its wider orientation (\texttt{portrait} forces the
taller one).
\item[margin] one length for all four margins. \texttt{hmargin} sets left and right
together, \texttt{vmargin} top and bottom.
\item[left, right, top, bottom] the four margins individually. Give two opposite margins
and the text size between them is the remainder; give one and the text keeps its width and
simply shifts.
\item[textwidth, textheight] (also \texttt{width}, \texttt{height}) the text block size
directly, rather than via the margins.
\item[centering] centre the text block on the sheet --- equal margins on each axis.
\texttt{hcentering} and \texttt{vcentering} centre one axis only.
\item[headsep, footskip] the running-head and running-foot gaps, passed straight through.
\end{description}

\section{Lengths}

Any option that takes a length accepts the engine's units --- \texttt{in}, \texttt{pt},
\texttt{cm}, \texttt{mm}, \texttt{pc} --- so \texttt{margin:2cm} and \texttt{margin:0.75in}
are both fine. A bare number is read as points.

\section{Composing}

Options combine left to right. A common book setup is a named sheet with a single margin,

\begin{quote}
\noindent\texttt{\\geometry paper:a5 margin:0.75in}
\end{quote}

\noindent and a slide-style page is a wide sheet turned on its side with a centred body,

\begin{quote}
\noindent\texttt{\\geometry paper:a4 landscape:on textwidth:9in centering:on}
\end{quote}

\noindent Because the settings are global, a later \texttt{\\geometry} call changes the
frame for the pages that follow it, the way \texttt{\\newgeometry} does in LaTeX.

\vfill\eject
